## Solution

We solve the homogeneous linear second-order PDE with constant coefficients

$$a\,z_{xx}+b\,z_{xy}+c\,z_{yy}=0,\qquad a,b,c\in\mathbb{R},\;a\neq 0.$$

### 1. Characteristic (ansatz) equation

Look for solutions of the form $z=\varphi(y+\lambda x)$, where $\varphi$ is an arbitrary twice-differentiable function of one variable and $\lambda$ is a constant to be determined. Then

$$z_x=\lambda\varphi',\quad z_{xx}=\lambda^{2}\varphi'',\quad z_{xy}=\lambda\varphi'',\quad z_{yy}=\varphi''.$$

Substituting into the PDE and dividing by $\varphi''$ gives the **characteristic (auxiliary) equation**

$$a\lambda^{2}+b\lambda+c=0.$$

Its roots are

$$\lambda_{1,2}=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{\Delta}}{2a},\qquad \Delta:=b^{2}-4ac.$$

The nature of the roots is governed by the **discriminant** $\Delta=b^{2}-4ac$, which simultaneously classifies the equation.

### 2. Case $\Delta>0$ — hyperbolic

Two distinct real roots $\lambda_1\neq\lambda_2$. Each gives an independent solution $\varphi_i(y+\lambda_i x)$, and by linearity the general solution is

$$\boxed{\,z=\varphi_1(y+\lambda_1 x)+\varphi_2(y+\lambda_2 x)\,},\qquad \lambda_{1,2}=\frac{-b\pm\sqrt{b^{2}-4ac}}{2a},$$

with $\varphi_1,\varphi_2$ arbitrary $C^2$ functions of one variable. The equation is **hyperbolic**.

### 3. Case $\Delta=0$ — parabolic

A repeated real root $\lambda=-b/(2a)$. The two independent solutions are $\varphi_1(y+\lambda x)$ and $x\,\varphi_2(y+\lambda x)$ (the factor $x$ is the standard remedy for a double root, exactly as in the ODE case). Hence

$$\boxed{\,z=\varphi_1(y+\lambda x)+x\,\varphi_2(y+\lambda x)\,},\qquad \lambda=-\frac{b}{2a},$$

with $\varphi_1,\varphi_2$ arbitrary $C^2$ functions. The equation is **parabolic**.

*Verification sketch.* Put $u=y+\lambda x$ and $z=x\,\varphi_2(u)$. Then $z_x=\varphi_2+\lambda x\varphi_2'$, $z_{xx}=2\lambda\varphi_2'+\lambda^{2}x\varphi_2''$, $z_{xy}=\lambda\varphi_2'+\lambda^{2}x\varphi_2''$, $z_{yy}=\lambda^{2}x\varphi_2''$. Substitution gives
$$a(2\lambda\varphi_2'+\lambda^{2}x\varphi_2'')+b(\lambda\varphi_2'+\lambda^{2}x\varphi_2'')+c\,x\varphi_2''=x\varphi_2''(a\lambda^{2}+b\lambda+c)+(2a\lambda+b)\varphi_2'.$$
Both coefficients vanish: $a\lambda^{2}+b\lambda+c=0$ (root) and $2a\lambda+b=0$ (double-root condition). $\square$

### 4. Case $\Delta<0$ — elliptic

Complex conjugate roots $\lambda=\alpha\pm i\beta$ with

$$\alpha=-\frac{b}{2a},\qquad \beta=\frac{\sqrt{4ac-b^{2}}}{2a}=\frac{\sqrt{-\Delta}}{2a}>0.$$

Equivalently $a\lambda^{2}+b\lambda+c=0$ gives $a(\alpha^{2}-\beta^{2})+b\alpha+c=0$ and $2a\alpha\beta+b\beta=0$, i.e. $b=-2a\alpha$ and $a\alpha^{2}+b\alpha+c=a\beta^{2}$.

The real general solution is obtained by taking real and imaginary parts of the complex solutions:

$$\boxed{\,z=\varphi_1(y+\alpha x)\cos(\beta x)+\varphi_2(y+\alpha x)\sin(\beta x)\,},$$

with $\varphi_1,\varphi_2$ arbitrary $C^2$ functions of one variable. The equation is **elliptic**.

*Verification.* Set $u=y+\alpha x$ and $z=F(u)\cos(\beta x)+G(u)\sin(\beta x)$. A direct differentiation gives
$$z_{xx}=\cos(\beta x)\bigl[F''\alpha^{2}-F\beta^{2}+2G'\alpha\beta\bigr]+\sin(\beta x)\bigl[G''\alpha^{2}-G\beta^{2}-2F'\alpha\beta\bigr],$$
$$z_{xy}=\cos(\beta x)\bigl[F''\alpha+G'\beta\bigr]+\sin(\beta x)\bigl[G''\alpha-F'\beta\bigr],\qquad z_{yy}=F''\cos(\beta x)+G''\sin(\beta x).$$
Collecting the coefficient of $\cos(\beta x)\,F''$: $a\alpha^{2}+b\alpha+c=a\beta^{2}$, which cancels with the $-aF\beta^{2}$ term. The coefficient of $\cos(\beta x)\,G'$ is $2a\alpha\beta+b\beta=0$. The $\sin$-terms cancel by the identical identities (with $F,G$ swapped in sign). Hence $a z_{xx}+b z_{xy}+c z_{yy}\equiv 0$. $\square$

### 5. Classification summary

| Discriminant | Roots of $a\lambda^{2}+b\lambda+c=0$ | Type | General solution |
|---|---|---|---|
| $\Delta=b^{2}-4ac>0$ | two distinct real $\lambda_1,\lambda_2$ | **hyperbolic** | $\varphi_1(y+\lambda_1 x)+\varphi_2(y+\lambda_2 x)$ |
| $\Delta=0$ | double real $\lambda=-b/(2a)$ | **parabolic** | $\varphi_1(y+\lambda x)+x\,\varphi_2(y+\lambda x)$ |
| $\Delta<0$ | complex $\alpha\pm i\beta$ | **elliptic** | $\varphi_1(y+\alpha x)\cos(\beta x)+\varphi_2(y+\alpha x)\sin(\beta x)$ |

In every case the general solution involves **two arbitrary twice-differentiable functions of one variable**, consistent with the PDE being second order.

### 6. Degenerate remark ($a=0$)

If $a=0$ the characteristic equation degenerates to the linear $b\lambda+c=0$. When $b\neq0$ there is a single root $\lambda=-c/b$ and the solution is $z=\varphi_1(y)+\varphi_2(y+\lambda x)$ (the term $\varphi_1(y)$ corresponds to the "lost" root at infinity, i.e. $z_{xx}=z_{xy}=0$). When $a=b=0$ the equation reduces to $c\,z_{yy}=0$, giving $z=\varphi_1(x)+y\,\varphi_2(x)$. These are the limiting/parabolic-like sub-cases of the same discriminant classification.

